# What the SDIST carries beyond the package itself.
#
# ⭐ WITHOUT THIS FILE THE RELEASED SDIST SHIPS A BROKEN TEST SUITE. setuptools' default sdist
# contents include `tests/test*.py` but NOT `tests/conftest.py`, so the tarball attached to every
# GitHub Release contained the tests and not the fixtures they depend on: two tests failed
# outright, and — the part that actually matters — the autouse fixture that makes "no test in this
# suite reaches the network" TRUE was simply absent from the artifact.
#
# That also quietly weakened the contract in README.md that "tests travel with the module", for
# anyone consuming the sdist rather than the repository. Tests that travel without their
# conftest have not travelled.
include MANIFEST.in
include README.md
include CHANGELOG.md
include pyproject.toml
include .leakguard.json
# PEP 561's marker, named here as well as in `pyproject.toml`'s `package-data`. ⭐ Measured: at
# setuptools 68 EITHER mechanism alone carries it into both artifacts, and at 84 it is an implicit
# data file that needs neither. They are floor insurance rather than one-per-artifact, which is
# what the comment here used to claim; the check that actually holds the line is the assertion
# both workflows make against the built wheel.
include src/kw_common/py.typed
recursive-include tests *.py

# ⭐ THE SETUP CONTRACT'S TWO FILES, and they are ARGUMENTS for the suite exactly as
# `.leakguard.json` is. `tests/test_alerting_env.py` reads `alerting.env.template` and checks it
# against the required-key manifest, and reads `docs/alerting-setup.md` and checks that it names
# the three variables, pipes nothing into a shell, and fetches the tag this package actually is.
# An sdist without them ships a suite that ERRORS — the same failure as the missing conftest, one
# release later. (The running count of this class is kept in ONE place, on the hook's entry
# below; two counters disagreeing is how the last one got to be wrong.)
#
# They are also the two files an operator most wants out of a tarball: the template is what gets
# copied to the shared root, and the document is what says where.
include alerting.env.template
recursive-include docs *.md

# ⭐ THE WORKFLOWS, because two tests READ them. `test_the_CI_workflow_runs_BOTH_scans_FROM_THE_
# INSTALLED_PACKAGE` and `test_the_required_status_check_names_are_recorded_in_the_workflow` open
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, so an sdist without it ships a suite that ERRORS — and this was the
# first occurrence of the class caught by unpacking the tarball and running the tests rather than
# by grepping its file list. (Running count: below, once.) `README.md`'s "tests travel with the module" is false for an sdist consumer
# otherwise, and the packaging job now runs the shipped suite instead of taking the claim on
# trust.
recursive-include .github/workflows *.yml

# ⭐ THE PRE-PUSH HOOK, because `tests/test_leak_guard_hook.py` COPIES IT and drives it against a
# real `git push`. An sdist without it ships a suite that errors on collection.
#
# ⚠️ THE RUNNING COUNT OF THIS CLASS, kept here and nowhere else so the two copies cannot drift:
# the missing conftest, `.leakguard.json`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, the setup-contract pair,
# and now the hook. FIVE times a file the SUITE depends on was left out of the tarball. That is
# why `ci.yml` and `release.yml` both unpack the sdist and RUN it rather than grepping its file
# list. It is also the file an sdist consumer would actually want: the check itself.
recursive-include .githooks *

# ⭐ `.leakguard.json` IS INCLUDED because it is an ARGUMENT for the tests, not decoration. This
# repository's own config is the worked example the leak-guard suite reads, and an sdist whose
# suite cannot find it ships tests that skip the one property the extraction exists to prove.
# Same failure as the missing conftest, one release later.
#
# ⛔ `scripts/` NO LONGER EXISTS, and the reason it was excluded is worth keeping because it now
# binds somewhere else. The guard used to be vendored there and was kept out of the sdist because
# its docstring enumerated the fleet's other repositories by name, which would have put that
# inventory inside every tarball attached to every public Release. The guard is now
# `src/kw_common/leakguard.py` — INSIDE the wheel, published to everyone who installs the
# library — so exclusion is no longer available as the fix.
#
# ⭐ AND EXCLUSION WAS NEVER THE REAL ANSWER, WHICH #16 MEASURED: `recursive-include tests *.py`
# put SEVENTEEN private service names into every published sdist, in four files, while the one
# test that asked the question looked at a single module. Keeping a file OUT of the tarball
# protects that file and nothing else. What keeps the names out now is `.githooks/pre-push`,
# running a guard that holds the list and is committed to no repository at all — and the
# `recursive-include .githooks *` above is what makes the hook travel with the source, so an
# sdist consumer gets the check rather than a reference to one.
